{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:16387657 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:16387657 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"16387657","source":"MED","pmid":"16387657","doi":"10.1016/j.molcel.2005.11.001","title":"Mechanistic insights into sulfur relay by multiple sulfur mediators involved in thiouridine biosynthesis at tRNA wobble positions.","authorString":"Ikeuchi Y, Shigi N, Kato J, Nishimura A, Suzuki T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ikeuchi Y","lastName":"Ikeuchi","firstName":"Yoshiho","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2829-3840"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Chemistry and Biotechnology, Graduate School of Engineering, University of Tokyo, 7-3-1 Hongo, Bunkyo-ku, Tokyo 113-8656, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Shigi N","lastName":"Shigi","firstName":"Naoki","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4452-8264"}},{"fullName":"Kato J","lastName":"Kato","firstName":"Jun-Ichi","initials":"J"},{"fullName":"Nishimura A","lastName":"Nishimura","firstName":"Akiko","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Suzuki T","lastName":"Suzuki","firstName":"Tsutomu","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9731-1731"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2829-3840"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9731-1731"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4452-8264"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"21","journalIssueId":1281273,"dateOfPublication":"2006 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2006,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Mol Cell","title":"Molecular cell","essn":"1097-4164","issn":"1097-2765","nlmid":"9802571","isoabbreviation":"Mol Cell"},"printPublicationDate":"2006-01-01"},"pubYear":"2006","pageInfo":"97-108","abstractText":"The wobble bases of bacterial tRNAs responsible for NNR codons are modified to 5-methylaminomethyl-2-thiouridine (mnm5s2U). 2-thio modification of mnm5s2U is required for accurate decoding and essential for normal cell growth. We identified five genes yhhP, yheL, yheM, yheN, and yccK (named tusA, tusB, tusC, tusD, and tusE, respectively) that are essential for 2-thiouridylation of mnm5s2U by a systematic genome-wide screen (\"ribonucleome analysis\"). Efficient 2-thiouridine formation in vitro was reconstituted with recombinant TusA, a TusBCD complex, TusE, and previously identified IscS and MnmA. The desulfurase activity of IscS is stimulated by TusA binding. IscS transfers the persulfide sulfur to TusA. TusE binds TusBCD complex and stimulates sulfur transfer from TusA to TusD. TusE also interacts with an MnmA-tRNA complex. This study revealed that 2-thiouridine formation proceeds through a complex sulfur-relay system composed of multiple sulfur mediators that select and facilitate specific sulfur flow to 2-thiouridine from various pathways of sulfur trafficking.","affiliation":"Department of Chemistry and Biotechnology, Graduate School of Engineering, University of Tokyo, 7-3-1 Hongo, Bunkyo-ku, Tokyo 113-8656, Japan.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"New Energy and Industrial Technology Development Organization","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Escherichia coli","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Sulfur","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"